/-!
# Connectedness bounds for complete local rings

Source: Stacks Project, Algebraization, Tag 0ECR (`lemma-punctured-still-connected`),
and Local Cohomology, Tag 0DX9 (`lemma-bound-cd`).
(blueprint: lem-per-complete-local-connectedness-bound)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace PrimeSpectrum

/-- Grothendieck's connectedness estimate for an ideal generated by `r` elements in a
nonzero complete Noetherian local ring of finite dimension. The numbers `c` and `d` are
specified as the least dimensions of closed subsets with disconnected complement and
of irreducible components, respectively. Connectedness includes nonemptiness.

The inequality is shifted by one to use `WithBot ℕ∞` without truncated subtraction:
for nonempty `T` of dimension `e`, `e + r + 1 < min (c + 1) d` means exactly
`e < min (c, d - 1) - r`, with the latter subtraction interpreted in the integers.
For empty `T`, its dimension is bottom, and the inequality holds, as in the source.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebraization, Tag 0ECR (`lemma-punctured-still-connected`),
and Local Cohomology, Tag 0DX9 (`lemma-bound-cd`).
(blueprint: lem-per-complete-local-connectedness-bound)

Proof, following the ten steps of the node:
1. Set `t = cd(R, I)`. The local-cohomology generator bound
   (`lem-per-local-cohomology-generator-bound`) gives `0 ≤ t ≤ r`. First prove the
   punctured assertion under the stronger assumption `t < min (c, d - 1)`.
2. If the puncture is empty, `V(I)` is just the closed point. Apply
   `lem-per-isolated-intersection-dimension-bound` to the whole spectrum to obtain
   `dim X ≤ t`, contradicting `d ≤ dim X` and `t < d - 1`.
3. Put `U = X` minus its closed point. Since `d > t + 1`, apply
   `lem-per-formal-punctured-idempotent-algebraization` to identify the colimit of
   reduced neighborhood section rings with the inverse limit of infinitesimal
   neighborhood section rings. Reduction preserves the underlying spaces, and a
   prime contains `I` if and only if it contains any positive power of `I`.
4. A separation of `U ∩ V(I)` into two nonempty clopen pieces gives compatible
   nontrivial idempotents on all infinitesimal neighborhoods, by parts (b) and (c) of
   `lem-weil-pbc-scheme-clopen-idempotent`. Transfer them through the ring isomorphism.
   Represent the resulting element on one neighborhood and shrink it until its
   idempotence holds there; filteredness follows by intersecting neighborhoods.
   The representative is neither zero nor one, since its reductions are neither.
   Part (b) of the clopen-idempotent correspondence yields a disconnected open `V`.
5. For `Z = X` minus `V`, one has `Z ∩ V(I)` equal to the closed point. The isolated
   intersection bound gives `dim Z ≤ t`, whereas the definition of `c` gives
   `c ≤ dim Z`. This contradicts `t < c` and proves the punctured assertion.
6. For the general assertion, empty `T` leaves the nonempty connected local spectrum
   `V(I) = Spec(R/I)`. Otherwise `T` contains the closed point, and its dimension is
   a nonnegative integer `e`. A disconnected complement is either empty or splits
   into two nonempty clopen pieces, using `def-weil-connected-scheme`.
7. Apply `lem-per-prime-avoidance-connectedness-slice` to `T`, with the empty family
   in the empty-complement case, and with the two pieces otherwise. It gives `e`
   elements in the maximal ideal cutting `T` down to the closed point and meeting
   each prescribed piece. The ideal `J` obtained by adjoining these to `I` is proper.
8. The puncture of `V(J)` equals the intersection of that slice with `V(I)` minus
   `T`. It is empty in the first case; otherwise the two intersections form a
   separation into nonempty clopen pieces. Thus this puncture is not connected.
9. The proper-sum bound in `lem-per-local-cohomology-generator-bound` gives
   `cd(R, J) ≤ t + e`. The contrapositive of steps 2--5 applied to `J` gives
   `min (c, d - 1) ≤ cd(R, J) ≤ t + e ≤ r + e`, contradicting the dimension bound.
10. The singleton closed point lies in `V(I)` and has dimension zero. The general
    assertion applied to this singleton gives the final punctured conclusion. -/
theorem complete_local_connectedness_bound
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [IsAdicComplete (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) R]
    (hfinite : ringKrullDim R < ⊤)
    (I : Ideal R) (hproper : I ≠ ⊤) (r : ℕ)
    (hgen : ∃ f : Fin r → R, Ideal.span (Set.range f) = I)
    (c d : ℕ)
    (hc : IsLeast {e : WithBot ℕ∞ | ∃ Z : Set (PrimeSpectrum R),
      IsClosed Z ∧ ¬ IsConnected Zᶜ ∧ topologicalKrullDim Z = e} (c : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hd : IsLeast {e : WithBot ℕ∞ | ∃ Z : Set (PrimeSpectrum R),
      Z ∈ irreducibleComponents (PrimeSpectrum R) ∧ topologicalKrullDim Z = e}
      (d : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    (∀ T : Set (PrimeSpectrum R), IsClosed T → T ⊆ zeroLocus (I : Set R) →
      topologicalKrullDim T + (r : WithBot ℕ∞) + 1 < (min (c + 1) d : WithBot ℕ∞) →
      IsConnected (zeroLocus (I : Set R) \ T)) ∧
    (r + 1 < min (c + 1) d →
      IsConnected (zeroLocus (I : Set R) \ {IsLocalRing.closedPoint R})) := by
  sorry

end PrimeSpectrum
